<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-scalar-multiples-of-metrics" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Scalar multiples of metrics
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Give an example of a real number <m> \alpha \in \R </m> and a metric <m> \rho \colon X \times X \to \R </m> so that <m> \alpha \rho </m> is not a metric.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <answer>
    <p>
      Any non-positive real number <m> \alpha \leq 0 </m> and any metric <m> \rho \colon X \times X \to \R </m> on a set <m> X </m> with at least two distinct points are correct answers.
    </p>
  </answer>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      Since <xref ref="lemma-metrics-are-non-negative"/> implies that metrics take only non-negative values, any non-positive real number <m> \alpha \leq 0 </m> and any metric <m> \rho \colon X \times X \to \R </m> on a set <m> X </m> with at least two distinct points are correct answers.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
